APS-013 — Policy Composition

Document ID: APS-013
Title: Policy Composition
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Composition Model (CPCM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Composition Model establishes the principles, mechanisms, and governance required to combine multiple independent Canonical Policy Objects into coherent, deterministic, and reusable policy compositions while preserving semantic integrity, modularity, and traceability.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Composition architecture
Composition semantics
Composition types
Dependency management
Evaluation order
Validation
Governance
Composition lifecycle

This specification applies to every composed policy set.

3. Canonical Definition

Policy Composition is the governed process of combining multiple independent Canonical Policy Objects into a unified policy composition while preserving the semantics, identity, governance, and traceability of each constituent policy.

Composition combines policies.

It SHALL NOT merge their identities.

4. Objectives

The Policy Composition Model SHALL:

Enable modular governance.
Promote policy reuse.
Preserve policy independence.
Support deterministic evaluation.
Enable scalable governance.
Preserve traceability.
Support interoperability.
Prevent composition ambiguity.
5. Composition Principles

Every composition SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Independence

Every constituent policy SHALL preserve its own identity.

Explicit Composition

Composition SHALL be explicitly declared.

Implicit composition SHALL NOT occur.

Determinism

Equivalent policy compositions SHALL produce equivalent evaluation behavior.

Traceability

Every composed policy SHALL remain individually traceable.

Modularity

Policies SHALL remain independently governable.

Explainability

Composition SHALL expose every participating policy.

6. Composition Types

Atlas defines the following canonical composition types.

Composition Type	Description
Sequential	Policies evaluated in order
Parallel	Policies evaluated independently
Conditional	Policies activated by conditions
Hierarchical	Parent composition governs child compositions
Composite	Multiple composition models combined

Each composition SHALL explicitly declare its type.

7. Canonical Policy Composition

Every Policy Composition SHALL define:

Property	Description
Composition ID	Unique identifier
Composition Name	Human-readable name
Composition Type	Canonical type
Member Policies	Constituent policies
Evaluation Order	Execution sequence
Dependencies	Required policies
Governance Domain	Responsible authority

Policy Compositions SHALL themselves be Canonical Atlas Objects.

8. Composition Members

Every member SHALL define:

Policy reference
Policy version
Membership role
Optionality
Priority
Activation conditions

Members SHALL remain independently versioned.

9. Dependency Management

Compositions SHALL support:

Required dependencies
Optional dependencies
Version compatibility
Circular dependency detection
Dependency validation
Dependency traceability

Circular dependencies SHALL prevent activation.

10. Evaluation Order

Atlas defines canonical evaluation orders.

Policy A
    │
    ▼
Policy B
    │
    ▼
Policy C

Or

      Policy A
        │
────────┼────────
│               │
▼               ▼
Policy B    Policy C
        │
────────┼────────
        ▼
 Final Decision

Evaluation order SHALL be deterministic.

11. Composition Validation

Every Policy Composition SHALL be validated for:

Structural correctness
Member completeness
Dependency integrity
Version compatibility
Conflict freedom
Governance consistency
Semantic consistency

Validation SHALL precede activation.

12. Composition Lifecycle

Every Policy Composition SHALL support:

Defined
    │
Validated
    │
Approved
    │
Published
    │
Active
    │
Modified
    │
Retired

The lifecycle SHALL remain independent of member policy lifecycles.

13. Composition Governance

Every composition SHALL define:

Composition owner
Steward
Review authority
Approval authority
Governance domain
Applicable policies
Audit requirements

Governance SHALL remain independently traceable.

14. Composition Quality

Every composition SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Consistency
Determinism
Explainability
Maintainability
Reusability
Scalability
Traceability

Quality SHALL be measurable.

15. Composition Events

Policy Composition MAY generate Canonical Events.

Examples include:

Composition Created
Member Added
Member Removed
Composition Activated
Composition Validated
Composition Retired

Events SHALL conform to ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV).

16. Conformance

A Policy Composition implementation conforms if it:

Supports canonical composition types.
Preserves member independence.
Implements deterministic evaluation.
Validates dependencies.
Detects circular references.
Supports governance.
Maintains traceability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Dynamic policy composition.
AI-assisted composition optimization.
Federated compositions.
Domain-specific composition templates.
Distributed composition orchestration.
Adaptive composition strategies.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical composition semantics.

